  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nInfluence from an upper trough in Canada spreads into the\nNorthern Plains and Upper Midwest, starting this afternoon into\nevening as a dry cold front sweeps through. This will increase\nwinds and bring today's above average temperatures back toward\naverage Thursday into next week. Winds may briefly gusty this\nevening, especially in the Red River Valley. Winds reinvigorate\narea-wide by late morning, lasting through the afternoon.\nNorth-northwest winds 20-35 mph can be anticipated, highest in\nnortheast ND and northwest MN.\n\nWhile cooler temperatures into the upper 30s are forecast \ntonight in northeast ND, a steady light breeze behind the front \nis expected to prevent temperatures from tanking via radiational\ncooling. This will limit the chance for frost.\n\nFrost becomes more likely Thursday night into Friday as surface\nhigh pressure centers itself more into our region keeping winds\ncalm and skies clear. Best chance for fog currently resides in\nnortheast ND and northwest MN, but areas generally north of \nI-94 also hold a medium chance of seeing frost.\n\nAdditional chances for frost exist this weekend into next week,\nparticularly Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nThe upper pattern remains in a quasi-zonal flow through the \nweekend, with a shortwave trough forecast to move through around\nSaturday. This will provide forcing for ascent bringing a medium\nchance for rain showers. This wave is likely to be devoid of\noverall moisture content, thus rain amounts are anticipated to\nbe rather low - less than quarter of an inch.\n\nEnsemble guidance suggests in transient shortwave ridging early\nnext week, although the influence of this remains in question.\nThere could be a brief trend in temperatures toward slightly\nabove average temperatures; otherwise, temperatures are forecast\nto remain near average.",
